※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `p.x ← v` writes one field of a record, written the way the field is read.
※
※ **Only the field is written.**  The rest of the record is not read, not copied
※ and not touched: a record that is somewhere has an address for each field, and
※ writing one is a store to that address and nothing else.  Which is why this
※ belongs to a record that is *somewhere* -- a name given storage of its own,
※ what a reference names, or a field of one of those.  A record the program
※ worked out is a value in registers and has no place for a field to be at
※ (4588); binding it to a name is what gives it one.
※
※ **A field is as mutable as the record holding it**, and a record reached
※ through a reference is as mutable as the reference.  The mark is written once,
※ where the thing was made or where it was lent, and a field does not get to say
※ it again: `p.x ← v` on a `p` defined without `mut` is refused (4004), and
※ `r⌖.x ← v` through a `&Point` is refused (4535).
※
※ **A field that is itself a record is written out where it stands**, for the
※ reason a definition's is: there is no register a value of one could be made in.
※
※ Compare: **C**, **Rust**, **Go** and **Zig**, where this is the same statement
※ written the same way and means the same thing; **ML**, whose records are
※ immutable unless a field says `mutable`, which is the other place the mark
※ could go; **Haskell**, which has no such statement at all and builds a new
※ record naming the fields that differ.

type Point = x : u32 ; y : u32
type Line = from : Point ; to : Point

@[impure]
fn move_to(l: &mut Line, x: u32):
    ※ through a reference, and a field of a field
    l⌖.to.x ← x
    ※ and a whole nested field written out where it stands
    l⌖.from ← Point(.x ← 0u32, .y ← 0u32)

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let l: mut Line = Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 1u32, .y ← 2u32),
                           .to ← Point(.x ← 3u32, .y ← 4u32))
    l.to.y ← 8u32
    move_to(&mut l, 7u32)
    if l.to.x ≠ 7u32:
        1u6
    ※ what the other write left alone
    elif l.to.y ≠ 8u32:
        2u6
    elif l.from.x ≠ 0u32 ∨ l.from.y ≠ 0u32:
        3u6
    else:
        0u6
